## Step 4 — Rate Limiting Audit
**Time: 1 hour | Blocks: production security**

Run this grep to find every mutation endpoint without a rate limiter:

```bash
grep -rL "rateLimit\|rateLimiter\|Ratelimit" apps/web/app/api/v1/**/*.ts
```

**Endpoints confirmed missing rate limiters — add to each:**

```typescript
// Pattern — add near top of each handler, before auth check:
import { Ratelimit } from '@upstash/ratelimit'
import { Redis } from '@upstash/redis'
import { rateLimitKey } from '@/lib/request'   // extracts IP from req

const limiter = new Ratelimit({
  redis: Redis.fromEnv(),
  limiter: Ratelimit.slidingWindow(10, '1m'),  // adjust per endpoint
  prefix: 'xxm:rl:endpoint-name',
})

// Inside handler:
const { success } = await limiter.limit(rateLimitKey(req))
if (!success) throw new RateLimitError()
```

**Per-endpoint limits:**

```
/auth/forgot-password      →  5  per 15m  (brute force protection)
/auth/verify-email         →  10 per 15m  (OTP retry limit)
/auth/register             →  already has limiter ✓
/mandates                  →  10 per 1h   (one mandate per user effectively)
/mandates/[id]/delay       →  5  per 1h
/admin/invitations         →  20 per 1h   (admin-only, higher limit)
/admin/notifications/broadcast → 5 per 1h (expensive, send to all members)
/admin/contributions/generate → 3 per 1h  (batch operation)
/contributions/pay         →  already has limiter ✓
```

**Done when:** Every `POST`, `PATCH`, `DELETE` endpoint has a rate limiter. `GET` endpoints do not need one unless they're computationally expensive (the reports CSV is the only one that might need it).

